    docs(TC-68): fix-1 GREEN — the marker is not engine-authenticated; state the threat model
    
    Closes the one codex §9 targeted-round-2 [P1]. Transcript:
    dev/plans/runs/codex/0.8.20/slice-22-review-round2-targeted-20260729T004445Z.log
    (Risks 1, 3 and 4 PASS — untouched.)
    
    The finding is accepted in full: an actor with write access to the SQLite file
    can compute the current fingerprint from public inputs, write it into
    _fathomdb_open_state, and force the early return Ok(()) before the 45-probe
    verification runs. The preceding test commit measured what that is worth. The
    remedy is prose, and deliberately so.
    
    WHAT WAS FALSE, AND IS NOW CORRECTED
    
      probe_verification_is_cached's rustdoc and §5 of the design note both claimed
      "the only `true` is an exact match against a value THIS ENGINE WROTE AFTER A
      VERIFICATION IT PERFORMED ITSELF". That is not true and cannot be made true:
      the engine cannot distinguish its own marker from an identical one. Both sites
      now say what a `true` actually means — the fingerprint inputs are unchanged
      since SOME engine recorded a pass.
    
    WHY NOT CRYPTOGRAPHY (design note §8.2)
    
      Every fingerprint input is derivable from the database file or the shipped
      binary. A salt or MAC key would live in the same file or binary and be read by
      the same actor. An embedded local-first engine has no key custodian and no
      separate trust domain. Any construction here raises the bar for a naive editor
      only, while leaving a shorter route open — theatre.
    
    THE MEASURED FACT THAT SETTLES IT (design note §8.3)
    
      a_forged_stored_baseline_defeats_the_probe_even_when_it_fully_runs: re-baseline
      _fathomdb_embed_probe.reference_vec to the drifted backend's own output, DELETE
      the marker, open. All 45 embeds run and the probe PASSES — it verified the
      drifted backend against a baseline that backend supplied. The control (same
      drift, un-forged baseline, marker cleared) is caught. The check body exercised
      is byte-identical to the slice baseline fe85337f. So the cache adds NO new
      attack surface; it changes when the probe runs, not who can lie to it.
    
    WHAT THE DOCS NOW SAY
    
