   313	## 8. THREAT MODEL — the probe is a self-check, not an integrity boundary
   314	
   315	**Added by fix-1**, closing the codex §9 targeted-round-2 `[P1]`
   316	(`dev/plans/runs/codex/0.8.20/slice-22-review-round2-targeted-20260729T004445Z.log`).
   317	
   318	### 8.1 The finding, accepted
   319	
   320	> `probe_verification_is_cached` trusts any `_fathomdb_open_state.value` exactly
   321	> equal to the freshly computed SHA-256. That is fail-safe for absent, corrupt,
   322	> truncated, non-TEXT or stale values because they miss and run the probe. It is
   323	> not fail-safe for an attacker-modified row: the fingerprint inputs are
   324	> deterministic, public DB and build inputs, not a secret or an engine-authenticated
   325	> marker, so a writer can forge the current digest and force the early
   326	> `return Ok(())` before the 45-probe verification runs.
   327	
   328	**True, and accepted.** `a_marker_matching_the_current_state_serves_a_drifted_backend`
   329	states it as an executable fact: on one workspace, with the marker absent the
   330	drifted backend is caught (45 embeds, `dense_disabled`); with the same digest
   331	written back it is served with 0 embeds.
   332	
   333	**§5's original wording was an over-claim and has been corrected.** It read *"the
   334	only `true` is an exact match against a value **this engine wrote after a
   335	verification it performed itself**"*. The engine cannot distinguish its own marker
   336	from an identical one written by anything else. What a `true` actually means is:
   337	**the fingerprint inputs are unchanged since *some* engine recorded a pass.**
   338	
   339	### 8.2 Why the remedy is prose, not cryptography
   340	
   341	There is nothing to authenticate the marker *with*. Every fingerprint input —
   342	identity triple, pinned `mean_vec`, compiled-in fixture, the two floors, the stored
   343	baseline blobs — is readable from the database file or the shipped binary. A salt
   344	or MAC key would live in the same file or the same binary and be read by the same
   345	actor. An embedded local-first engine has no key custodian, no separate trust
   346	domain and no privileged process. Any construction here would raise the bar for a
   347	*naive* editor only, while the *same* actor keeps a shorter route open (§8.3) —
   348	i.e. security theatre.
   349	
   350	### 8.3 The measured fact that settles it: the pre-slice path is equally forgeable
   351	
   352	`a_forged_stored_baseline_defeats_the_probe_even_when_it_fully_runs`:
   353	
   354	1. seed a verified workspace, then rewrite **every** `_fathomdb_embed_probe.reference_vec`
   355	   to the *drifted* backend's own output. Ordinals, `probe_text`, the identity
   356	   triple and the blob lengths are untouched, so the 0.8.18 fix-2 completeness
   357	   validation — which pins row *shape*, never blob *content* — sees nothing;
   358	2. **delete the cache marker**, so the cache demonstrably plays no part;
   359	3. open with the drifted backend. All **45** embeds run, and the probe **passes**:
   360	   it has verified the drifted backend against a baseline the drifted backend
   361	   itself supplied. Dense stays enabled; `search` does not refuse.
   362	
   363	The control leg — identical drifted backend, **un-forged** baseline, marker cleared
   364	— is caught and refuses dense, so the forgery is what does it.
   365	
   366	The code exercised in step 3 is the check body **as shipped at the slice baseline
   367	`fe85337f`**: `git diff fe85337f a39b865e -- src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/src/lib.rs`
   368	shows the only changes inside `probe_check_against_baseline_inner` are the doc
   369	comment, a type alias, the cache gate and the `record_probe_verification` call. The
   370	comparison logic is byte-identical.
   371	
   372	**Conclusion: the cache adds no new attack surface.** An actor with write access to
   373	the SQLite file already had a shorter route — and can in any case rewrite
   374	`canonical_nodes`, the `vector_default` vec0 rows and
   375	`_fathomdb_embedder_profiles.mean_vec` directly, which defeats retrieval outright
   376	without touching the probe at all. The probe was never a boundary against that
   377	actor; the cache changes *when* it runs, not *who can lie to it*.
   378	
   379	### 8.4 The boundary, stated plainly
   380	
   381	- **In scope — what the probe catches.** *Accidental* backend drift: the same
   382	  declared embedder moved CPU↔CUDA, rebuilt against a new library or driver, a
   383	  re-exported ONNX graph; plus a corrupt, partial or mangled stored baseline. That
   384	  is the ADR's purpose (`dev/adr/ADR-0.8.18-vector-equivalence-self-check.md`).
   385	- **In scope — the accident guarantee.** `R-VEQ-4` holds unchanged: every
   386	  unreadable, absent, garbled or stale cache state runs the probe rather than
   387	  trusting it (§5).
   388	- **OUT of scope — an actor with write access to the database file.** They can
   389	  forge the marker (§8.1), forge the baseline (§8.3), or rewrite the corpus and the
   390	  vectors. FathomDB's trust boundary is the file: a caller who lets an untrusted
   391	  party write the database has already lost the dense arm, the lexical arm and the
   392	  corpus. The equivalence probe is a **correctness self-check, not an integrity
   393	  boundary**, and must not be cited as one.
   394	- **What forging buys, bounded.** A digest is valid only for the state it was
   395	  computed over. `a_replayed_verdict_marker_is_defeated_by_a_mutated_baseline` and
   396	  `a_replayed_verdict_marker_is_defeated_by_a_rewritten_pinned_mean` show a known
   397	  digest expires at the next change to any fingerprint input, so a forgery must be
   398	  re-applied after every legitimate change.
   399	- **What the cache genuinely narrows** is §6 and only §6: same-identity backend
   400	  drift is no longer caught per-open. That is the ruled trade, and it is about
   401	  accident, not attack.
   402	
   403	### 8.5 Hardening considered and rejected
   404	
   405	- **Salt / MAC / keyed digest.** Rejected: §8.2 — no secret exists to key it with.

